--!nocheck
-- Real-mesh data for every gun & melee weapon archetype, sourced from the community
-- "Warfare Mesh Collection" (devforum). Each entry is {MeshId, TextureId, Scale} -- Scale is copied
-- verbatim from that pack's own SpecialMesh.Scale for this exact MeshId (cross-checked against the
-- source file entry by entry). Do NOT "fix" a small-looking Scale (e.g. the shotgun's -0.004) --
-- that was already tried once and was wrong: SpecialMesh.Scale on a MeshType.FileMesh is applied
-- directly to the mesh's own native vertex data and is NOT related to the parent Part's Size at all,
-- so a tiny Scale here just means that particular mesh was authored at a huge native scale. The
-- Part's Size (set in AimRngClient's MakeVmMeshPart) is a fixed, generous, purely-cosmetic-safety
-- constant now -- it has no effect on how the mesh looks, only gives the renderer margin.
--
-- Orient: the rotation that turns this mesh's raw axes into "barrel toward -Z, top toward +Y" (the
-- viewmodel's forward/up). Derived from the source pack itself: on its display racks every gun lies
-- barrel-toward-world(-X), upright, so each donor Part's rotation tells us exactly which of the mesh's
-- own axes is the barrel and which is up. Old "tool-handle" meshes (barrel along local +Y -- the ones
-- that rendered "held upright") come out as Rx(-90); modern ones (barrel along -Z) come out identity.
-- Bounds: that donor Part's Size (mesh extents in the mesh's own axes, at this Scale) -- only a fallback;
-- the server measures each mesh's exact size at startup (AimRngServer) and the client prefers that.
